  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 359 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nTwo upper level disturbances are moving toward Central MT this\nmorning, one from the south and one from the north. There is ample\namounts of precipitation coming up with the disturbance from the\nsouth this morning. This will be a quick moving system, with much\nof the precipitation exiting the region by early Saturday morning.\nSome of the precipitation could be heavy at times this afternoon.\nOverall, QPF is expected to range from about 1/2 inch to 3/4 of an\ninch over Central and Southwest MT, with lighter amounts as you \ngo northward towards the US/Canadian border. \n\nThere are two main concerns with this event.\n\nOne, strong winds. Expect strong wind gusts later this morning and\nthrough early this evening over portions of North Central MT.\nThus, no changes to the high wind warning area.\n\nThe second concern is when does the rain change to snow. The\nfreezing level was over 11,000 feet on Thursday evening. The\nforecast models have this freezing level lowering very quickly\nthis afternoon, as the heavy precipitation brings down the colder\nair from aloft. This is possible, but also the airmass might be so\nsaturated at lower levels, it doesn't adiabatically cool as\nefficiently as the forecast models have progged. Thus, the only\nchange to the winter weather advisory was to add a few more\nmountain ranges over Southwest MT. Otherwise, for most lower\nelevation locations, the rain should change to snow by this\nevening, but with the majority of the precipitation through by \nthis time, snow accumulations at lower elevations will be on the \nlow side for this event.\n\nTemperatures today will be much cooler than they have been, with\nmost areas having high temperatures this morning, and falling\ntemperatures this afternoon.\n\nAfter this event, expect rather dry conditions for Sun/Mon, with\nthe cool temperatures continuing. For Tue/Wed, the next weather\nsystem approaches the CWA, with the best chance for precipitation\nover the Central and Southwest portions of the CWA. The airmass\nwill be quite a bit colder by the middle of next week, that much\nof the precipitation will fall as snow, with some rain mixing in\nat lower elevations.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe main concern with the rain/snow today is what time does the\nrain change to snow? The quicker it changes to snow, the higher\nprobability of a bit higher snow accumulations. Since this will be\na quick moving system, impacts from the snowfall will short. That\nbeing said, some snowfall rates could approach 1 inch per hour\nthis afternoon/evening...which could affect the evening commute.\n\nAlso, I can't rule out a lightning strike here/there...but the\nprobability is to low to include thunder for most areas right now.\nBrusda",
